          "ruleId": "CWE-400",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Declared dependency body-parser is pinned with range ^1.19.0 (>=1.19.0 <2.0.0), which permits resolving to versions below 1.20.3 -- versions in that range carry a known denial-of-service advisory (CVE-2024-45590) related to inefficient handling of URL-encoded request bodies. This is a components-with-known-vulnerabilities finding independent of request flow; note the exploit/CVE corpus tool was unavailable this session (returned 'not indexed'), so this is grounded only in the queried version string plus prior knowledge of the advisory -- recommend the verifier confirm the CVE/version match independently.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently: package.json (and the CpgDependency node) both confirm body-parser is declared at range \"^1.19.0\" (>=1.19.0 <2.0.0), which can resolve to any pre-1.20.3 1.x release. CVE-2024-45590 (body-parser DoS via inefficient URL-encoded body handling, fixed in 1.20.3) genuinely affects that range per my training knowledge; exploit_search confirms the corpus is unindexed (\"not indexed\") so that part of the analyst's statement is also verified as accurate, not fabricated. No lockfile is present in the repo, so the exact resolved version cannot be pinned -- consistent with the analyst's own caveat. However, the analyst mischaracterized/omitted a material fact that changes real-world risk: body-parser is listed under `devDependencies`, not `dependencies` (package.json line 37, inside the devDependencies block starting line 36). Grepping the whole tree shows body-parser is only required in test/test.js (test harness setup) and mentioned in README usage examples; the package's actual shipped code (index.js) never requires or uses body-parser. Since this project (express-xss-sanitizer) is a published npm library, devDependencies are never installed for downstream consumers and this code path is never part of the library's own runtime attack surface -- the DoS-prone parser only ever processes requests generated by the project's own test suite in a dev/CI environment, not attacker-controlled traffic against a deployed instance. This is a real but low-impact SCA finding (dev-tooling hygiene), not an exploitable production vulnerability."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "package.json",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
