        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-1104",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Declared devDependency eslint is pinned at ^7.20.0, a major version line (ESLint 7) that is end-of-life and no longer receives security/bugfix patches from upstream. Low impact since it is a lint-only dev dependency not present in the runtime attack surface of the two scanned source files, but flagged per the outdated/EOL component class.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently verified: package.json devDependencies declares \"eslint\": \"^7.20.0\" (line 39), and the graph's Dependency node matches exactly (name=eslint, version=^7.20.0). Grepping the whole source tree shows eslint is never required/imported by runtime code \u2014 its only appearances are the devDependency/script entries in package.json and inline `/* eslint-disable ... */` lint-directive comments in test/test.js, confirming it is a lint-only dev tool with no presence in the library's runtime attack surface. ESLint's major-version support policy (each major line is maintained only until ~6 months after the next major ships) means v7 (released 2020, superseded by v8 in Oct 2021) has been out of upstream security/bugfix support for years \u2014 this EOL fact is well-established public knowledge about the project's own release policy, not something requiring a Metasploit-style exploit record. exploit_search was attempted but the corpus is unavailable in this session (\"not indexed\"), so exploitability could not be cross-checked against a weaponized module either way \u2014 but this finding is a component-hygiene/EOL claim rather than a specific-vulnerability claim, so that gap does not block confirming the underlying fact pattern. Severity is correctly scoped LOW given dev-only, non-runtime exposure."
          },
